EN_SHOPPING = """
<p>Leaving Marrakech empty-handed is almost impossible — and leaving with a carpet you paid three times over is very easy. Between the two lies a Medina of remarkable craftsmen, a few simple rules and a few traps. Here is what we explain to guests at the <strong><a href="/en/">Riad Mylaya</a></strong> before their first trip into the souks: where to buy what, how to spot quality, which prices are reasonable and how to bargain without falling out with anyone.</p>
<p><em>The prices below are ranges observed in the Medina in September 2026, for good-quality pieces, after bargaining. They vary enormously with size, material and shop: treat them as a bearing, not a tariff.</em></p>

<h2 id="where">Where to buy: souks, fondouks, cooperatives, concept stores</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>The Medina souks</strong> — north of Jemaa el-Fna, organised by trade: Semmarine (fabrics, jewellery), Attarine (spices, perfumes), Cherratine (leather), Haddadine (ironwork), Sebbaghine (dyers), Chouari (wood). Prices are lowest here and bargaining is expected.</li>
<li><strong>The fondouks</strong> — former caravanserais turned into workshops around a courtyard. You see the craftsman at work, which is the best guarantee of authenticity.</li>
<li><strong>Cooperatives</strong> — for argan oil and Atlas carpets, with fixed prices and verifiable working conditions. Slightly more expensive, no surprises.</li>
<li><strong>New-town concept stores</strong> (Guéliz, Sidi Ghanem) — contemporary Moroccan design, fixed prices, card payment, shipping available. Two to three times souk prices, for a tighter selection and complete peace of mind.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="what">What to bring home, and what it should cost</h2>

<h3 id="carpets">Carpets</h3>
<p>The biggest commitment. Three main families: <strong>Beni Ouarain</strong> (thick undyed wool, black diamonds, Middle Atlas), flat-woven <strong>kilims</strong>, lighter and cheaper, and <strong>boucherouites</strong>, made from recycled offcuts, colourful and often the most affordable. <em>Expect roughly 800 to 2,500 MAD for a small kilim, and 3,000 to 12,000 MAD and well beyond for a large hand-knotted Beni Ouarain.</em> Check the back: knots should be regular and visible, wool should smell of wool, and a handmade carpet never has two perfectly identical halves. A serious seller will let you photograph it, measure it and sleep on the decision.</p>

<h3 id="leather">Leather and babouches</h3>
<p><em>Plain babouches: 100 to 250 MAD. Embroidered or lined: 250 to 450 MAD. Goatskin bag: 400 to 1,200 MAD. Poufs (sold unstuffed): 300 to 700 MAD.</em> Smell the leather: a strong chemical smell means poor industrial tanning. Pull on the stitching. And beware of sizes, which are approximate — try them on.</p>

<h3 id="spices">Spices, argan and cosmetics</h3>
<p><em>Real ras el-hanout sells for roughly 30 to 80 MAD per 100 grams; Moroccan saffron from Taliouine for 30 to 60 MAD per gram</em> — be wary of "saffron" at 20 MAD a sachet, which is turmeric or safflower. Culinary argan oil (roasted, hazelnut-flavoured) and cosmetic argan oil (cold-pressed, almost odourless) are two different products: <em>150 to 300 MAD per 100 ml in a cooperative</em>. Beldi black soap, rhassoul and rose water cost a few tens of dirhams and make the best gifts — they are also what you will meet at the <a href="/en/blog/marrakech-hammam-spa-wellness">hammam</a>.</p>

[[CTA]]

<h3 id="metal">Lanterns, ironwork, trays</h3>
<p><em>Small cut-iron lantern: 150 to 400 MAD. Large lantern: 600 to 1,500 MAD. Engraved brass tray: 400 to 1,200 MAD.</em> Brass is recognisable by weight and sound; painted tinplate is much lighter. Ask whether a lantern is wired for European electricity — it often is not.</p>

<h3 id="ceramics">Ceramics, tadelakt and zellige</h3>
<p>Cooking tagines (raw, unglazed clay) are not the same as serving tagines, decorated and glazed: <em>80 to 250 MAD for a serving dish, 150 to 400 MAD for a Salé clay cooking tagine</em>. Fès and Safi bowls, zellige tiles and tadelakt objects travel well if properly wrapped. Have them wrapped on the spot, and carry them in the cabin if you can.</p>

<h3 id="textiles">Textiles, kaftans, blankets</h3>
<p><em>Cotton or viscose scarf: 80 to 200 MAD. Cashmere-blend scarf: 300 to 600 MAD. Sequinned wedding blanket (handira): 1,200 to 3,000 MAD. Embroidered kaftan: from 600 MAD, far more made to measure.</em> The "cactus silk" (sabra) sold everywhere is almost always viscose: pretty, but not silk, and it should not be paid for at silk prices.</p>

<h2 id="bargaining">How to bargain, properly</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Visit three shops before buying.</strong> It is the only way to learn what something really costs.</li>
<li><strong>Let the seller name a price first</strong>, then offer 40 to 50% of it, with a smile.</li>
<li><strong>Aim to settle around 60 to 70%</strong> of the opening price for everyday crafts. On carpets the gap can be much wider.</li>
<li><strong>Only bargain for what you genuinely want.</strong> Haggling and then walking away is seen as disrespectful.</li>
<li><strong>Tea commits you to nothing.</strong> Accept it if you feel like it, decline politely if not.</li>
<li><strong>Stay courteous and keep the pace slow.</strong> Bargaining is an exchange, not a fight; humour works better than firmness.</li>
<li><strong>Pay cash, in dirhams.</strong> Cards are rare in the souks, and "the shop rate" is always bad.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="traps">The classic traps</h2>
<ul>
<li>The "guide" you meet in the street who takes you to his cousin: his commission is inside your price.</li>
<li>The "free" tannery visit with a complimentary sprig of mint, which ends in a leather shop at triple prices.</li>
<li>Saffron and argan oil that are too cheap: they are not what they claim.</li>
<li>The "cooperative's last day" and other invented deadlines.</li>
<li>"Antique" and "Berber" pieces made in series — a genuinely old object has a coherent price and a precise history.</li>
<li>Shipping promised by the seller: prefer an international carrier you choose yourself, or take the piece with you.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="practical">Practical: hours, luggage, customs</h2>
<p>Souks open around 9-10am and close around 8-9pm; many craftsmen close on Friday at prayer time, and Sunday is quieter. Weigh your purchases: carpets and poufs fill a suitcase fast, and excess baggage costs more than the bargain you won. At the airport, liquids — argan, rose water, olive oil — must go in checked luggage. And keep cash in dirhams: souks almost never take cards. The Medina is five minutes' walk from the riad, and we are happy to keep your purchases at reception during the day. See also our <a href="/en/blog/marrakech-prices-budget">prices and budget guide</a>.</p>
"""

